/** Standalone batch AC -- new 4-case standard (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress).
 * All solvers below were independently written and verified byte-for-byte against each problem's
 * real official Sample before being trusted to generate boundary/tricky/stress expected output,
 * EXCEPT uva-673 whose official Sample was corrupted (see below).
 *
 * uva-630-anagrams-ii: group vocabulary words by their sorted-letters key; a test word's anagrams are
 *   every vocabulary word sharing that key (including the word itself, if present in the vocabulary),
 *   listed in vocabulary-input order with a 3-char-wide sequential number.
 * uva-657-the-die-is-cast: two-level flood fill -- first find each maximally-connected non-background
 *   (either '*' or 'X') region as one die, then within each die's own pixel set find each maximally-
 *   connected 'X'-only region (4-connected, diagonal doesn't count) as one dot; output sorted dot
 *   counts per throw.
 * uva-665-false-coin: an "=" weighing confirms every coin in it genuine (the false coin, being a real
 *   weight difference, could never balance an equal-count all-genuine pan against another); an
 *   unequal weighing instead proves the false coin lies somewhere in that weighing's coin set. The
 *   answer is the single coin surviving (a) exclusion of every confirmed-genuine coin and (b)
 *   intersection with every unequal weighing's coin set -- 0 if more than one candidate remains.
 * uva-673-parentheses-balance: THE SCRAPED OFFICIAL SAMPLE CLAIMS EVERY ONE OF ITS 35 TEST STRINGS IS
 *   "No", including several that are unambiguously balanced by hand-tracing the standard bracket-
 *   matching stack algorithm (e.g. "[(()[][])[[][][]]]" is a clean, fully-nested match) -- this is
 *   corrupted/placeholder data, not a real judge transcript. Verified instead with small
 *   hand-constructed balanced/mismatched/unmatched-leftover cases. sampleLimit: 0.
 * uva-674-coin-change: classic unbounded-knapsack change-counting DP (1/5/10/25/50 cent coins),
 *   precomputed once up to the stated max 7489; verified against the problem's own worked example
 *   (11 cents -> 4 ways).
 * uva-679-dropping-balls: recursive halving -- the i-th ball entering a subtree goes left if i is odd,
 *   right if even (since the flag simply alternates per visit), becoming the
 *   ceil(i/2)-th ball within whichever subtree it entered; right-subtree results are offset by that
 *   subtree's own leaf count. Verified against the problem statement's own fully-worked example
 *   (depth 4, balls 1/2/3 -> positions 8/12/10).
 * uva-686-goldbach-s-conjecture-ii: sieve to just under 2^15, count UNORDERED prime pairs (a<=n/2,
 *   both a and n-a prime) per even n -- the ordered-vs-unordered distinction (a<=n/2 rather than
 *   a<n) is exactly what the problem asks for ("not... as two different pairs").
 */
